---
import { btnCls, cardCls, checkCls, crumbCls, selectCls, tabCls, tabsCls } from '../lib/ui';
// Discoveries (#/discoveries): the tricks of the trade, one table. A trick is what a model can do,
// judged from outside by its bets (thorp/dash/tricks.py KNOWN_TRICKS; the ones Thorp measures are in
// fleet.TRICKS). A row opens the trick (#/discoveries/<trick>): the stars needed to learn it (the
// mechanisms inside, thorp/dash/heads.py), the size the field learns it at, and how Thorp is
// tracking, class by class, toward learning it at a smaller class. The star chart and the traits
// are one link away (#/discoveries/stars, #/discoveries/traits).
---
<div class="section" id="discoveries">

  <!-- ============================== the tricks of the trade -->
  <div class="dview" data-dview="index">
    <h1>Tricks of the trade</h1>
    <nav class={tabsCls('line', 'subnav')} data-orientation="horizontal" data-variant="line" id="discnav"><a class={tabCls('')} data-state="inactive" data-dsub="two-facts" href="#/discoveries">Chaining two facts</a><a class={tabCls('')} data-state="inactive" data-dsub="quantum" href="#/discoveries/quantum">Quantum mechanics</a><a class={tabCls('')} data-state="inactive" data-dsub="registry" href="#/discoveries/registry">Registry of tricks</a></nav>

    <!-- the trick in focus: how Thorp proves a model chains two facts (the heart of the leaderboard, and of the claim) -->
    <div class="dsub" data-dsub="two-facts">
      <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel tfclaim')}>
        <h2>The claim</h2>
        <p class="tflead">A Thorp model <b>a tenth the size</b> of each 2–4B model chains two facts as well as it does. Chaining two facts is the trick: two premises, neither of which states the conclusion, put together into one that no sentence on the shelf states. This is how Thorp proves a model can do it, and how the claim is judged.</p>
        <div class="tfstatus" id="tfstatus"></div>
      </section>

      <!-- the methodology, as written in fleet.METHODS["chaining"] (rendered as written there, never rewritten here) -->
      <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel method')} id="method">
        <h2>Methodology: <span id="mname">chaining two facts</span> <span class="note" id="mstatus"></span></h2>
        <div class="k">The trick</div>
        <p class="mtrick" id="mtrick"></p>
        <div class="row mrow">
          <div class="mcol">
            <div class="k">Its parts <span class="s">and whether today's tests cover each</span></div>
            <div class="scroll"><table id="mparts" data-nosort><thead><tr><th>part</th><th>what it means</th><th>covered</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
          </div>
          <div class="mcol">
            <div class="k">The gaps <span class="s">what the tests cannot yet rule out</span></div>
            <ul class="mgaps" id="mgaps"></ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="k mt">The controls <span class="s">planned: the logic deck</span></div>
        <div class="scroll"><table id="mcontrols" data-nosort><thead><tr><th>control</th><th>what it does</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
        <div class="k mt">The metric</div>
        <p class="mmetric" id="mmetric"></p>
        <div class="k mt">The cards it needs <span class="s">the stars that do the trick: in the deck (measured; pulsing when lit on any sailing) or planned</span></div>
        <div class="cards mcards" id="mcards"></div>
      </section>

      <h2 class="tfhead">How it is measured and judged today</h2>

      <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel tfstep')}>
        <div class="tfn">1</div><div class="tfb">
        <h2>The test: matched pairs</h2>
        <p>Each chaining test is 24 pairs. A pair is a passage that ends mid-sentence, then two endings matched in length and plainness: <b class="good">the idea</b>, which follows only if the two facts in the passage are put together, and <b class="bad">a rival</b>, which reads as naturally but does not follow. The passage is split into its <b>premise</b> (the facts) and its <b>frame</b> (the closing words the ending hangs on). There are four chaining tests, one on each of the bettor's chains, 96 pairs in all; a model bets on every pair and never sees any of them in training.</p>
        <div class="scroll"><table class="pairs" id="tfexamples" data-nosort><thead><tr><th>chain</th><th>the passage: premise / <b>frame</b></th><th>the idea</th><th>its rival</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel tfstep')}>
        <div class="tfn">2</div><div class="tfb">
        <h2>The measure: lift, what the premise adds</h2>
        <p>For each ending the model's surprise is read in <b>bits per byte</b> (so tokenisers of different sizes compare). Its <b>preference</b> is the rival's surprise less the idea's: positive means it bets on the idea. But an ending can win on its words alone (a commoner word, a frame that leans one way), so the same preference is read again after <b>the frame alone</b>, and taken away:</p>
        <div class="tfformula">lift = [ bpb(rival | premise + frame) − bpb(idea | premise + frame) ] − [ bpb(rival | frame) − bpb(idea | frame) ]</div>
        <p>So <b>lift is what the premise adds</b>: zero for a model that only knows which words go together, positive only when the facts before the frame move the bet toward the idea. The <b>share right</b> is the fraction of pairs whose lift is above zero.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel tfstep')}>
        <div class="tfn">3</div><div class="tfb">
        <h2>Performed: the mark and the share</h2>
        <p>A test is <b>performed</b> when, at the same reading, the mean lift is at least <b id="tfthr">0.1</b> <i>and</i> at least <b id="tfshare">two in three</b> pairs are right, so a few large lifts cannot carry a model that is guessing on the rest. The untrained model, same seed, is the <b>floor</b>; untrained and small models sit within about <b id="tfnoise">0.03</b> of zero, which is the <b>noise</b>: nothing inside it counts as a reading.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel tfstep')}>
        <div class="tfn">4</div><div class="tfb">
        <h2>The test is graded before it is trusted</h2>
        <p>Each chaining test is the fifth rung of a chain of six (keel to bettor), and a chain is <b>graded</b> only when it gets harder at every rung across a ladder of outside models (Pythia 14m to 410m, Qwen3 0.6B to 4B): no model passes a rung while failing one below it, fewer pass each rung than the one below, and every rung is passed by someone. A test that a small model passes and a large one fails is a broken test, not a discovery. The 2–4B models the claim is judged against <b>never grade the test</b> they are scored on.</p>
        <div class="tfgrades" id="tfgrades"></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel tfstep')}>
        <div class="tfn">5</div><div class="tfb">
        <h2>The score: the chaining lift</h2>
        <p>A model's <b>chaining lift</b> is its mean lift on the four chaining tests. It is the one number <a href="#/leaderboard">the leaderboard</a> ranks every model on, outside and Thorp alike, read on the same 96 pairs. Below the 50m, where a class is not yet expected to chain, its progress is its <b>climb</b>: on each chain the rungs performed from the bottom, plus the next rung's share of its mark, the mean of the four (0 to 5).</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel tfstep')}>
        <div class="tfn">6</div><div class="tfb">
        <h2>The forecast: from the classes Thorp can train to the size it can't</h2>
        <p>The Mac cannot train a 400M model, so the claim is a forecast. Each Thorp class's best run gives one point, chaining lift against parameters, and two curves are fitted in log parameters: a <b>straight line</b> (conservative) and a <b>bending curve</b> (probable, since chaining bends upward with size in the outside models). Each is read at a tenth of each competitor's size. A <b>bootstrap</b> (1,000 draws, resampling the pairs and the seeds) gives a 5–95% band and <b>P</b>, the share of draws at or above the competitor's chaining lift. No forecast is made until <b>three classes</b> are above the noise: a curve through noise extrapolates nothing.</p>
        <p>The method is <b>backtested</b> where the answer is known, fitting the smaller outside models to predict the larger:</p>
        <div id="tfbacktest"></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel tfstep')}>
        <div class="tfn">7</div><div class="tfb">
        <h2>The rule, pre-registered</h2>
        <p>The claim is <b>made</b> when the forecast is trusted (three classes above the noise) and P ≥ 0.9 on the straight line for <b>every</b> competitor in the 2–4B range. It is <b>probable</b> when only the bending curve reaches 0.9. Anything less is not claimed. The rule was written down before the readings (DECISIONS.md, "Smoking Qwen, pre-registered"), so it cannot bend to them.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <!-- the long road: seven waypoints to quantum mechanics, and the bettor's four discovery chains on it -->
    <div class="dsub" data-dsub="quantum">
    <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel')}>
      <h2>The route to quantum physics <span class="note">seven waypoints, each resting on the one before; a waypoint's gate tricks (◆) are the ones a ship must perform before it moves on</span></h2>
      <div class="route" id="trickroute"></div>
    </section>
    <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel')}>
      <h2>The discovery chains <span class="note">the bettor's four tests (the Origin, energy, Mendeleev's gaps, the eternal return), each reached in six rungs, one per class, keel to bettor; the last row says what a model at each rung is, according to the four. Every class is scored on every rung; each cell is every class's best lift and best share of pairs right; ✓ is performed under the share rule (lift at 0.1 and two pairs in three right, at the same reading), ▲ passed below its class, ⚠ passed below its class while a bigger class fails it, so the pairs want auditing; Qwen3 0.6B for scale. Under each chain's name, its grade: graded when it gets harder at every rung across all the models read (no model passes a rung it fails below, fewer pass each rung, every rung reached)</span></h2>
      <div class="handtools"><label><input class={checkCls('')} type="checkbox" id="chainrefidx"> show the reference ladder <span class="note">(Pythia 14m to 410m and Qwen3 0.6B to 4B, read on the same pairs)</span></label></div>
      <div class="scroll"><table id="chaintable" data-nosort><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
    </section>
    </div>

    <!-- every trick known, measured or not -->
    <div class="dsub" data-dsub="registry">
    <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel')}>
      <h2>The tricks <span class="note">a trick is what a model can do, judged from outside by its bets; open one for the stars that do it and Thorp's progress, class by class. "The field shows it at" is the size band (one order of magnitude, in parameters) where the trick is documented in standard models, not a proven smallest size; hover a band for the detail. "Thorp's goal class" is the class of ship expected to reach it, about an order of magnitude smaller than the field where the depth it needs allows; ◆ marks a gate, performed before a ship moves on to the next waypoint</span></h2>
      <div class="handtools"><span class="note">waypoint</span><select class={selectCls('')} id="trickfamily"><option value="">all</option></select><span class="note">the field shows it at</span><select class={selectCls('')} id="trickband"><option value="">any size</option></select><span class="note" id="trickcount"></span></div>
      <div class="scroll"><table id="tricktable" class="clickable"><thead><tr><th>waypoint</th><th>trick</th><th>what it does</th><th>the field shows it at</th><th>how sure</th><th>stars needed</th><th>Thorp so far</th><th>Thorp's goal class</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
      <p class="note mt">Also: <a href="#/discoveries/stars">every kind of head (the star chart)</a> · <a href="#/discoveries/traits">traits</a></p>
    </section>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================== one trick -->
  <div class="dview" data-dview="trick">
    <h1><a class={crumbCls('back')} href="#/discoveries">← discoveries</a> <span id="tricktitle"></span> <span id="trickstate"></span> <span class="q" id="trickdoes"></span></h1>
    <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel')}><div class="rungover" id="trickover"></div></section>
    <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel')}>
      <h2>The stars needed to learn it <span class="note">the mechanisms inside thought to do this trick, each with where it is expected and its state in Thorp</span></h2>
      <div class="scroll"><table id="trickstars"><thead><tr><th>kind of head</th><th>what it does</th><th>rests on</th><th>expected from</th><th>first lit in</th><th>in Thorp</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
    </section>
    <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel')}>
      <h2>How Thorp is tracking, class by class <span class="note">each class of ship, how many of its sailings have been measured on this trick and how many performed it; the target is a tenth of the field's size</span></h2>
      <div class="scroll"><table id="tricktrack"><thead><tr><th>class</th><th>parameters</th><th>against the field</th><th>sailings measured</th><th>performed</th><th>best</th><th>first performed</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
    </section>
    <div class="row" id="trickcharts">
      <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel')} id="trickchartpanel">
        <h2>The trick, sailing by sailing <span class="note">its reading at every step, one line per sailing, coloured by class; the amber dotted line is the mark that counts as performed, the grey one the floor (an untrained ship), and labelled lines are outside models</span></h2>
        <div class="chart tall" id="trickchart"></div>
      </section>
      <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel')} id="trickpairpanel" hidden>
        <h2 id="trickpairtitle">Beside it</h2>
        <div class="chart tall" id="trickpairchart"></div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================== one chain: a bettor's test told as a climb, keel to bettor (fleet.CHAINS) -->
  <div class="dview" data-dview="chain">
    <h1><a class={crumbCls('back')} href="#/discoveries">← discoveries</a> <span id="chaintitle"></span> <span id="chainstrand"></span></h1>
    <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel')}><div class="rungover" id="chainover"></div></section>
    <div class="handtools"><label><input class={checkCls('')} type="checkbox" id="chainrefpage"> show the reference ladder <span class="note">(Pythia 14m to 410m and Qwen3 0.6B to 4B beside each step's evidence)</span></label></div>
    <div id="chainsteps"></div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================== the stars -->
  <div class="dview" data-dview="stars">
    <h1><a class={crumbCls('back')} href="#/discoveries">← discoveries</a> The stars <span class="q">what inside does it? attention heads that do one recognisable job; a star is lit when one head reaches its threshold, and lit shared when the best layer's two strongest heads reach it together; some rest on others</span></h1>
    <div class="handtools"><button class={btnCls('outline', '')} data-essay="nevermore">Read: what a Nevermore is</button></div>
    <div class="cards" id="starcards"></div>
    <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel')}>
      <h2>The bridge, layer by layer <span class="note">the bridge card is a curve over layers, not one head: how strongly the answer's position carries the middle term two premises share (the slowest deer), read by the soundings at each layer, in nats; one line per run that read it, coloured by class; the dotted line is the provisional threshold. Two hops need it resolved in the middle layers, before the second hop: resolved too late, the chain fails</span></h2>
      <div class="chart" id="bridgechart"></div>
    </section>
    <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel')}>
      <h2>The star chart <span class="note">every kind of attention head described so far. Those Thorp measures carry their state from the ledger; the rest are charted but not yet measured, each with how Thorp could measure it. "Rests on" is the dependency its source reports; a blank means none reported. "Expected from" is the smallest class predicted to reach it (the floor is depth: one hop per layer), set beside the smallest class where a sailing has lit it</span></h2>
      <div class="handtools"><span class="note">family</span><select class={selectCls('')} id="headfamily"><option value="">all</option></select><span class="note" id="headcount"></span></div>
      <div class="scroll"><table id="headtable"><thead><tr><th>kind of head</th><th>family</th><th>what it does</th><th>rests on</th><th>expected from</th><th>first lit in</th><th>in Thorp</th><th>first described</th><th>seen in</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================== the traits -->
  <div class="dview" data-dview="traits">
    <h1><a class={crumbCls('back')} href="#/discoveries">← discoveries</a> Traits <span class="q">can a ship's hidden state tell fiction, verse, first person or a language family apart on books it has never read; found at the threshold, learned when it also clears the floors</span></h1>
    <div class="handtools"><button class={btnCls('outline', '')} data-essay="traits">Read: how traits are found</button></div>
    <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel')}><div class="scroll"><table id="traittable" class="clickable"><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></section>
  </div>
</div>
